WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:05.500
The teasers for Shazam! promised a lighthearted,
laugh-filled adventure, and had everyone hoping

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:09.680
the film could bring some brightness to the
mostly gloomy DC Extended Universe.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:14.380
Now the reviews for Shazam! are officially
in, and we finally know whether the film soared

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:14.960
or sank.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:16.320
Spoiler alert!

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:21.220
With a 92 percent fresh score on Rotten Tomatoes
as of the making of this video, it's pretty

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:24.920
clear that Warner Bros. managed to capture
some real magic this time around.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.480
Let's dive in and see what the critics had
to say.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:31.760
Too many films seem to forget that people
often go to the movies because they want to

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:32.260
have fun.

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:36.219
Well, thank goodness for Shazam!, because
critics agree that the movie has plenty of

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:37.300
fun, and then some.

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:39.350
ScreenCrush's Matt Singer said,

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:41.140
"It's an escape from your troubles.

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:42.680
You feel good when it's over."

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:44.710
And Forbes contributor Mark Hughes said,

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:49.820
"The film is such a feel-good family-friendly
joyously exciting and amusing production,

00:00:49.829 --> 00:00:53.519
people will keep recommending it to their
friends and loved ones, and many will return

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.850
for second and maybe even third viewings."

00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:00.780
ComingSoon.net's Alan Cerny, meanwhile, compared
Shazam! to a warm, happy puppy.

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:03.359
"Shazam! invites everyone to have a good time.

00:01:03.359 --> 00:01:05.880
It doesn't have a mean-spirited bone in its
body.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:09.660
Shazam! has a big, goofy smile on its face,
and you can't help but smile back.

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:11.380
This is a puppy dog of a movie."

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:13.660
And Beatrice Verhoeven of TheWrap said

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.520
"Shazam! is such an unexpected joy from start
to finish at a time when Hollywood is choking

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:23.100
to death on all the superhero films that studios
keep ramming down its throat, here comes one

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:26.840
that looks at the genre in a different light
and reconceives it on a human level."

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:29.280
"I'd like to purchase some of your finest
beer, please!"

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:40.950
Speaking of that human level, the movie isn't
just fun, it's also surprisingly moving thanks

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:45.780
to a story that's as much about Billy Batson's
search for belonging as it is superheroics.

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:48.899
Alex Abad-Santos at Vox wrote that the film
understands

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:53.619
"...that humor and comedy are ways to approach
sensitive ideas, like children talking about

00:01:53.619 --> 00:01:57.479
feeling unwanted after being abandoned by
their parents, or what it's like to live with

00:01:57.480 --> 00:01:58.479
a disability."

00:01:58.680 --> 00:01:59.300
He added,

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:01.640
"Humanity is what Shazam is fluent in.

00:02:01.659 --> 00:02:05.920
And if we're lucky, Shazam assures us, we
can share a little bit of that magic, our

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:06.920
magic, with others."

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:12.460
At the heart of the film are three great performances
that critics are universally praising: Zachary

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:17.190
Levi as The Big Red Cheese, Asher Angel as
his younger alter-ego Billy Batson, and Jack

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:20.480
Dylan Grazer as Billy's new foster brother
Freddy Freeman.

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:22.580
The Hollywood Reporter's Frank Scheck wrote,

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:27.410
"Levi is a delight in the central role, hilariously
conveying the goofy adolescent within the

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:29.820
strapping body of his musclebound superhero."

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:32.860
Variety critic Owen Gleiberman agreed, saying,

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:37.640
"Zachary Levi brings off something so winning
it's irresistible … [he] uses that innocent

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:41.920
quality to take the superhero stuffing out
of the material — and to let Shazam discover

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:46.120
his identity in a way that makes this the
freshest origin story in some time."

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:48.280
As IGN's Jim Vejvoda said,

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:51.800
"Zachary Levi was born to play this superpowered
man-child."

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:55.830
As for the rest of the young cast, the Associated
Press's Lindsay Bahr wrote,

00:02:55.830 --> 00:03:01.310
"The young actors assembled here are astounding,
and immediately captivating, especially Grazer

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:05.550
as Freddy [...] Grazer and Levi are perfectly
matched for the job [and Grazer] is beyond

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:07.480
his years with his ability to draw a laugh."

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:11.900
Frank Scheck of the Hollywood Reporter agreed
that the chemistry of the cast made the film,

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:12.440
writing

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:17.340
"Angel and Grazer work together beautifully
as the teenage boys bonding over their joy

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:18.600
at discovering Shazam's powers."

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:19.880
"I'm right here, you can't see me?!"

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:20.840
"Where?"

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:23.480
"I'm invisible!

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:25.160
Over here, I'm right here!

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:26.940
Hahaha!"

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:32.140
Leading into the film, one area of concern
for fans was director David F. Sandberg, who

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:37.020
made his name not in comedy or superhero films,
but rather with the horror movies Lights Out

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:38.260
and Annabelle: Creation.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:41.960
So would he be able to pull off such a drastic
shift in tone for Shazam!?

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:43.880
According to critics, the answer is yes.

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:47.540
Forbes contributor Mark Hughes summed up the
critical consensus, saying,

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:52.520
"Director David F. Sandberg delivered a near-perfect
distillation of everything a Shazam movie

00:03:52.521 --> 00:03:58.110
should be, with the precise nuances and aesthetics
and performances to achieve the tone and style

00:03:58.110 --> 00:03:59.840
the film needed in order to succeed.

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:04.220
I cannot praise enough the performances he
got from his cast, and his overall ability

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:09.130
to make such a relatively smaller-scale personal
film still feel big, bold, and epic."

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:14.320
Of course, nothing can be perfect, and while
critics are mostly happy with Shazam!, there

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:17.329
are a couple things about the film they feel
could have been done better.

00:04:17.329 --> 00:04:19.480
Like, for instance, there could have been
less of it.

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:21.400
Frank Scheck at The Hollywood Reporter wrote,

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:26.710
"What should have been a fun, fast-paced 105
minutes or so is dragged out to a butt-numbing

00:04:26.710 --> 00:04:27.940
132."

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:31.420
And Digital Spy critic Hugh Armitage felt
similarly, saying,

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:36.120
"The finale drags on longer than it needs
to, but Shazam! is so much fun that it's a

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:36.620
minor complaint."

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:41.040
The other focus of complaints is the villain,
Dr. Sivana, played by Mark Strong.

00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:44.949
While most critics felt Strong did the best
he could with what he was given to work with,

00:04:44.949 --> 00:04:46.360
it seems he wasn't given much.

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:48.640
Lindsey Bahr at The Associated Press wrote,

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:53.840
"Dr. Sivana is woefully underwritten […] although
Strong does his best being the straight, serious

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:54.340
guy.

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:58.520
For the most part it comes across as less
of a threat and more of a buzzkill that gets

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:01.360
in the way all the fun we were having with
Freddy and Shazam."

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:05.360
Fans and critics alike seem thrilled at the
idea that Shazam!

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:10.640
Could usher in a fun new era of lighter movies
in the previously dire DC Extended Universe.

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:12.920
"Find him.

00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:17.540
Save Martha!"

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:18.360
"I'm Batman!"

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:19.200
"Get him, Batman!!"

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:23.780
Fandango managing editor Erik Davis wrote
in his early reaction to Shazam! that it's

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:26.260
"unlike anything DC has done before."

00:05:26.380 --> 00:05:29.180
And Digital Spy's Hugh Armitage agreed, saying:

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:34.800
"Aquaman hinted a bold, new approach to DC's
movies that doesn't try to emulate the MCU's

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:35.480
successes.

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:39.840
Shazam! makes good on that promise, waving
goodbye to the grim-faced Batfleck years,

00:05:39.850 --> 00:05:41.909
leaving us excited about what they'll do next.

00:05:41.909 --> 00:05:42.920
The future looks bright."

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:43.700
How bright?

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:45.180
Mark Hughes at Forbes wrote,

00:05:45.199 --> 00:05:50.210
"One of the best DC superhero origin films
ever made, Shazam! is also one of the best

00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:52.949
and most fun superhero films from any company."

00:05:52.949 --> 00:05:54.560
Now that sounds like true magic!

